  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Widespread rain showers, high elevation snow and breezy\n  conditions today\n\n-  Drying and warming conditions by mid-week.\n\n-  Trending wetter late Thursday into Friday.\n\nA low pressure system moving along the northwest Montana \nand Canadian border will slowly shift eastward today. This \nsystem is producing widespread rain showers across all of western\nMontana and north-central Idaho, with heavier showers focusing on\nnorthwest Montana and north-central Idaho today. Snow levels \ndropped to roughly 6,500 feet overnight, resulting in light snow \naccumulations from the Continental Divide southward to the \nBitterroots. Additionally, breezy westerly winds will develop once\nagain across western Montana, with gusts of 20 to 30 mph. \nBackcountry recreationists should prepare for cold, raw conditions\nand a heightened risk of hypothermia today.\n\nAs the system exits by tomorrow morning, the region will \nexperience a brief warming and drying trend through mid-week, \nthough isolated showers may linger near the Canadian border. \nClearing skies early Tuesday will cause temperatures to drop \nsignificantly, bringing chilly conditions to areas along and south\nof Interstate 90. Subfreezing temperatures are expected in the \nhigher valleys of southwest Montana and along the Continental \nDivide, while frost will likely develop in the typically colder \nlocations of the Missoula, Bitterroot, and Clearwater valleys.\n\nForecast models indicate another low-pressure system will move \ninland from the Pacific coast Wednesday night, tracking across \nwestern Montana and north-central Idaho Thursday into Friday. This \nincoming system is expected to interact with abundant moisture from \nthe south, leading to increasing precipitation and thunderstorm \nchances late in the week, particularly for areas south of Interstate \n90. While a majority of forecast models (nearly 80%) support a \nwetter scenario, confidence regarding the exact timing and specific \nlocal impacts remains low at this time.",
